In Python an if‑else construct lets you run one block of code when a condition is true and a different block when it is false. You start with the keyword if followed by a condition and a colon. The indented statements under the if are executed only if the condition evaluates to true. After those statements you can add the keyword else followed by a colon; the indented statements under else are run when the condition is false. For example, with a = 5 and b = 8, the code:

if a > b:
 print("a is greater than b")
else:
 print("b is greater than a or they are equal")

will print the second message because the condition a > b is false. The else part therefore provides an alternative action when the if condition does not hold.
